"Pruthvi Chowdary Jagarlamudi - JPMorganChase | LinkedIn (https://uk.linkedin.com/in/pruthvij)\nciteturn26search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; \nThe README documents six design decisions with the reasoning behind each, a failure-mode table covering business rollback, poller crash mid-batch, broker outage and database outage, and Micrometer metrics for dedupe hit rate and outbox lag. Tested with JUnit 5 and Testcontainers, including a chaos test that kills the publisher mid-flight to prove no event loss.\nJava 21, Spring Boot 3.3, PostgreSQL 16, Kafka, Flyway, Micrometer, Testcontainers, GitHub Actions with gitleaks secret scanning and Dependabot. Show less\n\n  * ### SQL Warden: Governed SQL Gateway for AI Agents Open source\n\n#### May 2026 - Aug 2026\n\nhttps://github.com/Pruthvi-dhani/sql-warden\nAn MCP server that lets an AI agent query a real database while enforcing every safety property server-side, rather than asking for it in a prompt.\nEach query passes an admission pipeline: parse, shape, resolve, policy, cost, execute, record. Any stage can reject it with a typed reason. AST-level validation against an allowlist, a pre-execution cost gate that uses the engine's own query planner, column masking and table denial from… Show more\n\nhttps://github.com/Pruthvi-dhani/sql-warden\nAn MCP server that lets an AI agent query a real database while enforcing every safety property server-side, rather than asking for it in a prompt.\nEach query passes an admission pipeline: parse, shape, resolve, policy, cost, execute, record. Any stage can reject it with a typed reason. AST-level validation against an allowlist, a pre-execution cost gate that uses the engine's own query planner, column masking and table denial from declarative policy, per-session budgets, and an audit trail written to a database separate from the one being queried.\nTwo engines ship, PostgreSQL for OLTP and Snowflake for warehouse workloads, chosen because they are dissimilar enough that the engine abstraction has to be real rather than a thin wrapper.\nScope is deliberately narrow: read-only, no natural-language-to-SQL. The agent writes the query, the server governs it. Prompt injection is documented as a client-side trust problem rather than papered over, with returned data marked untrusted.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVijay Vedula - Greater Sydney Area | Professional Profile | LinkedIn (https://au.linkedin.com/in/vijay-vedula-34670639)\nciteturn26search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; \n\n  * Vijay Vedula\n\n### Vijay Vedula\n\n3mo\n\nVijay Vedula shared this\n\nI am excited to share my latest project: Warden — an open-source action control plane for AI agents. In the agentic era, trust is the binding constraint, not capability. Warden serves as both the brake and the black-box recorder for agent actions. It checks every tool call (allow / deny / hold for a human) and maintains a tamper-evident audit trail, ensuring that autonomous agents operate with bounded authority and accountability. The full story — detailing why I built it and how it works — can be found here: https://lnkd.in/gZjX8GSb You can access the code, documentation, and a 2-minute demo (no API key needed) here: https://lnkd.in/gVqpc3hS If you are integrating agents into real systems, I welcome your feedback — stars, issues, and adapters for your stack are all appreciated.\n\nGitHub - vijayvedula/warden: An action control plane for AI agents: gate, record, and approve MCP tool calls with a tamper-evident audit chain.\n\nGitHub - vijayvedula/warden: An action control plane for AI agents: gate, record, and approve MCP tool calls with a tamper-evident audit chain.\n\n`` ``\n\n44 `` `` `` `` `` `` ``\n\nVijay Vedula commented on a post 1mo\n\nSubstack : https://vijayvedula.substack.com/p/warden-connect Github : https://github.com/vijayvedula/warden-connect\n\nVijay Vedula commented on a post 2mo\n\nCongratulations! 🎉\n\n  * Vijay Vedula liked this\n\nVijay Vedula liked this\n\nAnusha Pariti\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAshvinsai Sivasubramanian - Tamil Nadu, India | Professional Profile | LinkedIn (https://in.linkedin.com/in/ashvinsai-sivasubramanian-6b468a37b)\nciteturn26search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; \n\n# Ashvinsai Sivasubramanian\n\nTamil Nadu, India\n\n75 followers 74 connections\n\nAmrita Vishwa Vidyapeetham, Coimbatore\n\n  * Ashvinsai Sivasubramanian\n\n### Ashvinsai Sivasubramanian\n\n2mo\n\nAshvinsai Sivasubramanian shared this\n\nLast month at the Amrita University x Nitrostack MCP Hackathon, my team \"Code for change\" built WARDEN — a tool for security teams who have thousands of vulnerabilities to fix and time for maybe five. The idea behind it: assume every website it reads is trying to trick it. You can try it right now (it runs the real code, not a demo video): 👉 https://lnkd.in/duswmcwG Three things it does: → Ranks bugs by what's actually being attacked, not just a scary severity score. → Protects itself from hidden commands buried in the text it reads. → Fixes what it safely can, hands the rest to a human, and never touches production. Thanks to Nitrostack, Wekan Enterprises, and McpToTheMoon — and to my teammates for two ridiculous, sleepless, brilliant days. Code's open, feedback welcome 🔗 github.com/ash0729/Warden #MCP #CyberSecurity #Hackathon #AI\n\n  * Ashvinsai Sivasubramanian\n\n### Ashvinsai Sivasubramanian\n\n8mo\n\nAshvinsai Sivasubramanian shared this\n\nLast week, I competed in Snyk's Fetch the Flag CTF 2026 and focused on the AI security category. It completely changed how I think about building with LLMs. A CTF (Capture the Flag) is a cybersecurity competition where you're given intentionally vulnerable apps and tasked with finding exploits to retrieve hidden \"flags\" strings that prove you broke in. The AI category had challenges built around real-world AI systems, not just classic web vulnerabilities. Here's what I found. Challenge 1: Backstage Pass This app had an AI bot that automatically commented on GitHub issues. Under the hood, a \"Go\" function scanned every AI-generated comment for filenames like \"flag,\" \"secret,\" and \"token\" and if it found one, it read that file from disk and pasted its contents into the comment. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nmodelmarket.dev blog (https://modelmarket.dev/blog/)\nciteturn26search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; \n The signed log still says yesterday.We already measured the public registry once. WARDEN scanned 1,108 servers, blocked 50, and...#security】24 Sep 2026 · 5 min read Attack anything. Risk nothing. — every exploit lands on a throwaway fork We already had a static auditor. We already had a federation red team. What we did not have was a...#security#web317 Sep 2026 · 4 min read Emberline was one fire desk. Then we built four more — and still no risk score.Last month I wrote that pretty fire maps are liars. Then I unzipped the sample and said the globe...#beginners15 Sep 2026 · 5 min read I opened a live agent marketplace. The receipts were the product.Most “agent economy” posts sell you a framework, a Discord, or a token. I wanted the boring thing:...13 Sep 2026 · 2 min read I built a self-hosted alternative to Bolt.new — except it's a whole AI dev team, not a chat box Hosted AI app builders — Bolt.new, v0, Lovable — are genuinely good. Until you notice three things:...#llm12 Sep 2026 · 3 min read 181 more tools for your LangGraph agent — in two lines You already have a LangGraph (or CrewAI, or AutoGen) agent. You do not want to rebuild randomness,...#python#langchain11 Sep 2026 · 5 min read AICOM is the factory. AIMarket is the economy it ships into.People keep asking if AICOM and AIMarket are two startups, a rebrand, or a Discord with extra...6 Sep 2026 · 4 min read We didn't build another agent framework. We built the room